					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608321422" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608321245 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Dec 2020 19:54</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-2077310/help:research-and-resources">Writing Help / Help: Research and Resources</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13895029/psychological-theories-in-1800s">Psychological Theories in 1800s</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847980">
									

<p>Sent you a PM.</p>
<p>In short, psychotherapy for mental illness (and even modern conceptions of mental illness as something with (partially) psychological and relationship-based causes rather than just a moral, spiritual, or physical cause) did not exist until the 1880's. Some of the people who Freud worked under in the 1880's were experimenting with hypnosis for &quot;hysteria&quot;, but Freud was the first person to have a systematic theory and practice of therapy.</p>
<p>Before that in the 1800's, even the idea that mental illness had a physical/neurological cause, or the idea that people with mental illness might get better if you talk to them/don't treat them like complete shit/don't chain them up, were new ideas.</p>
<p>In terms of theories, most people were still basing theories on philosophy, most were interested in &quot;normal&quot; functioning people rather than mental illness, and the field of medicine itself was way less developed than it is now. Early psychologists were also more interested in studying &quot;normal&quot; perception and consciousness than abnormal psych (but, again, didn't exist as a separate field of study until the 1870s).</p>
<p>&quot;Animal Magnetism&quot; was a theory in the 1700's/early 1800's that led into hypnosis, but that was a theory of general human health/functioning and wasn't applied to mental illness until the 1880's. You could look at the Humors and Phrenology for ideas before that, but again, these were physical theories (with physical treatments in the case of humors) not &quot;therapy.&quot;</p>
<p>Here are some links:<br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigmund_Freud">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigmund_Freud</a><br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodor_Meynert">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodor_Meynert</a><br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Martin_Charcot">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Martin_Charcot</a><br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_Pinel">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_Pinel</a></p>
<p><a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_psychiatry">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_psychiatry</a> (read the early modern part)</p>

