<p>Seeking Greenlights: Yes<br />
Page Type: SCP Article<br />
Genre: Horror<br />
Page Format: I plan to use the standard page format, adding in interviews and audio logs to tell the stories of three separate cases.</p>
<p>Elevator Pitch: An auditory cognitohazard that latches onto the brain waves of a person until the afflicted person hears a “catchy&quot; song. Once the afflicted has a song stuck in their head, the anomaly perverts the lyrics to encourage the afflicted to act out intrusive thoughts. The anomaly can lie dormant for up to three days without a song to embed itself into, before eventually dissipating. The anomaly can be detected through basic brain activity scans, appearing in the shape of an insect on images of the brain. If a song is introduced that the afflicted finds catchy, the anomaly will stay undetected by the afflicted for two more days. During which it will play the song excessively in their mind. Anytime the afflicted sings the song, their brainwaves are modified to transmit the anomaly into the minds of others, starting a separate cycle. After the two day infection period, the anomaly begins to goad the original afflicted. The more the afflicted resists, the harder it pushes with symptoms ranging in severity. The Foundation can eradicate the anomalous brain activity by playing music that lacks (in the opinion of the listener) pleasant rhythm or rhyme, for 18 hours.</p>
<p>It is unclear if this anomaly is merely a phenomenon or an entity. No evidence of an entity occupying the physical body of any afflicted people exist, other than vivid descriptions of a singing insect-like creature seen during the REM cycles of the afflicted, in the first several days of exposure. The vivid and consistent descriptions of this creature, coupled with the insect-shaped brain image, have lead to a dichotomy in the observing staff. Those who believe it is merely a phenomenon, which the evidence seems to support the most, have developed the current SCP protocols. A separate team, continues to search for evidence of what they call the “Jukebug.”<br />
Central Narrative: Multiple accounts of different instances of the “Jukebug,” including: the aftermath of a child's birthday party, a surgeon, and a musician working on their newest song.</p>
<p>Hook: I think the idea that you could have been infected by this SCP and it’s already too late is spine-chilling and compelling enough to draw people in.</p>
<p>Additional Notes: Since the anomaly spreads due to divergent brainwaves of a person during the recitation of the song in their mind I am not sure if this is actually an auditory cognitohazard or not.</p>

