									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6391595&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608321422" alt="Quadraginta" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6391595)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;" >Quadraginta</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608319027 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Dec 2020 19:17</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13929691/awaiting-greenlight-the-book-of-the-siners">Awaiting Greenlight / The Book of the Siners</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847958">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I think I can very quickly summarize this as, 'generic biblical-themed pay-for-your-sins anomalous punishment object.' It falls into the Series 1 trope of being a thing that does a thing and exists for seemingly no reason other than what it is.</p>
<p>The <strong>Central Narrative</strong> is a very long extension of the <strong>Elevator Pitch</strong>, which is where the anomalous description is supposed to go. Because of this, this SCP essentially has no story.</p>
<p>I'm very much fascinated by religious and spiritual imagery and themes as well as the psychological aspect of it all. The idea of an SCP that makes people own up to their sins or get #rekt otherwise is some that innately appeals to me, as it portrays humanity in its purest, rawest form. However, this is as generic as generic gets. It might as well be God descending on random people and telling them to git gud or git fucked.</p>
<p>Apologies if this was harsh. To be fair, the anomaly works. It's <em>kinda</em> bland, but almost any anomaly can work nowadays if you attach a good enough story to it.</p>
<p>Tell you what. Here's a suggestion. You know what would make this work? Showing us someone going through its trials and tribulations. Considering how character-focused this anomaly innately needs to be, make a character(s), flesh them out, and have them undergo its trials. Forget test logs. This thing doesn't need them. One or two people will be enough to show off its obvious effects.</p>
<p>Show us someone at their lowest low before they come up. Or, ya know, having then go even further down. I think two characters would be best for this since you'd want to show us the good path and the bad path.</p>
<p>Anywho, I hope I helped and I bid you a good day. 👍</p>
</div>
